STEPHANIE WONG: With almost
500 sessions on Next 2019,

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:04.930
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:07.370
So if you didn't see the
high performance network,

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:09.120
then stay tuned because
here's the rewind.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.922
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.239
It's no surprise that
Google serves billions

00:00:16.239 --> 00:00:18.850
of search query answers
and hours of YouTube videos

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:20.770
to users all over the world.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.320
But what really
powers this ability

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.070
is the network that
allows us to replicate

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:26.690
content and services globally.

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:29.800
And it's also the network
that powers Google Cloud.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.140
The big question is how we
built the underlying physical

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:33.760
and operating aspects
of this network

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:35.800
to build for resilience.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.380
Let's first talk about our
global physical network.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.180
Over the last 20
years, Google has

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.490
built an extensive network
connecting continents

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:44.320
using submarine cables.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:46.270
In fact, one of our
new undersea cables

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:50.260
between Sydney and Singapore
has cut network latency in half.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:52.420
Planning and building
our own submarine cables

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:54.700
means we can build a
level of path certainty

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.400
and predictability of
network traffic performance.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.800
These paths are private,
and Google implements this

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.900
through our consortium
of partners.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:03.520
This means that
Google Cloud customers

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:05.740
are able to connect to
this network at locations

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:07.790
all over the world.

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:09.580
How do we plan for resilience?

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:12.220
We do extensive simulations
when deciding to add new network

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.350
capacity and make sure
it's not affecting

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:16.450
our SLOs in a negative way.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:18.642
We use trended historical
data on outages,

00:01:18.642 --> 00:01:20.350
and then classify
different failure types

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:22.720
across the entire network
path, and run them

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.220
for a fictional 20, 30, 40, 50
years across a range of network

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:28.400
paths.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.820
This gives us a reasonably
high degree of certainty

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:33.130
that the network we choose
and the additional capacity

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.410
locations and paths
we add will increase

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.390
the resilience and
the predictability

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:39.940
that we care so much about.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:41.980
These simulations
mean we can be sure we

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:45.470
are avoiding any negative impact
for Google Cloud customers.

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:47.732
How do we operate in
a resilient manner?

00:01:47.732 --> 00:01:49.690
We've been operating a
software defined network

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:51.760
across our core
data center network.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.770
We're now extending that
out to the very edge

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:57.280
with the announcement of
Espresso a couple of years ago.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.650
This enables us to maintain
a level of performance

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:02.410
and availability right
out to the edge, which

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:04.720
isn't possible using
regular routing protocols

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.280
and approaches.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:07.960
At the Edge Network,
we can connect

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.570
with nearly all significant
network operators, which

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:13.030
allows handoff of
network traffic directly

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:16.600
and reliably to
99% of our traffic.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:18.970
We also have network
site reliability teams

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:20.950
stationed around the world.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:23.770
Their role is to respond
and adapt to network events

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:26.950
to improve systems and
processes so reliability

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:30.490
and performance of network
continues to improve over time.

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:33.370
When an issue occurs,
they identify root causes

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:35.560
and implement new workarounds.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:37.870
In other words, they're
here as first responders

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:39.610
to make sure Google
Cloud customers have

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:42.480
a consistent and
reliable experience.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.000
Now, there's always a risk of
bad actors or network exposure.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.880
Google is continually
enhancing our own monitoring

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.330
and filtering capabilities
to get quicker and better.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.160
Because this is a
community-wide issue,

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.230
we have a new
partnership with MANRS,

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:59.560
which is a Mutually Agreed Norms
for Routing Security, where

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.780
we are working to enhance
the security of routing

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:02.725
on the internet.

00:03:02.725 --> 00:03:04.600
If you're wondering
about how Google designed

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:06.330
its strong network
backbone, we've

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:09.400
built a software defined
network from the ground up.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:12.820
Our first iteration was B4, a
massive backbone between all

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.273
of our data centers.

00:03:14.273 --> 00:03:16.690
We then introduced Jupiter,
which is our massive switching

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:18.430
fabric that goes
within our data centers

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:20.830
and makes tens of
thousands of machines

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:23.650
act together as one big system.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:26.620
Andromeda, the network software
defined at the virtualization

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:28.480
layer that Google
Cloud customers use

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.440
to build Cloud resources
and Espresso are taking SDN

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:34.420
capabilities out to the edge.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:36.850
All of these components have
helped us scale and deliver

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:40.000
traffic for Google products,
like Search and YouTube.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.430
And these are the same building
blocks that Google Cloud

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:43.870
customers get to use too.

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:45.460
Now, there is a
ton more they cover

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:46.960
in the talks about
the protocols we

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.030
use to improve performance,
our global load

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:52.600
balancer, virtual private cloud,
and logging and monitoring.

